#---- attaching_a_manifested_cran_package_keeps_typo_detection_alive
# The shape three separate adoption reviews reported: one `library()` of a
# popular package used to switch unresolved detection off for the whole project.
# A shipped export manifest makes the export set knowable, so real names resolve
# and a typo beside them is still a typo.
library(stringr)
shout <- function(x) str_to_upper(x)
oops <- function(x) str_to_uper(x)
#++++
Any
shout: <T> fn(x: T) -> Unknown
oops: <T> fn(x: T) -> Unknown
353..364 warning[unresolved] I could not resolve `str_to_uper` in this package, its imports, or builtins. Did you mean `str_to_upper`?

#---- a_meta_package_activates_the_members_it_attaches
# `library(tidyverse)` re-exports almost nothing and attaches its members
# instead, so activating them is what matches R — and it gives the project the
# members' typed declarations, not just their names: dplyr's verbs are
# class-preserving here.
library(tidyverse)
#: fn(frame: data.frame) -> integer
wrong <- function(frame) frame |> filter(cyl > 4) |> mutate(ratio = 1)
#++++
Any
wrong: fn(frame: data.frame) -> integer
331..376 error[type-mismatch] expected `integer`, found `data.frame`

